<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Network_Test_Config.xml: optional connection check and latency measurements on the System information page.
  Off by default (Enabled="False"). Only administrators can change this file; users cannot add or change targets.

  The measurements run in the context of the user, only read, and send no data other than a name lookup,
  an ICMP echo request to the default gateway and the opening (and immediate closing) of a TCP connection.
  They run in the background together with the other system information. The whole run stops after 15 seconds;
  targets that were not reached by then are shown as "not measured" and do not count.

  Result of the check (System information, category Network)
    Four rows with the state of each group (reachable, partly reachable, not reachable, not measured) and one
    sentence that tells what is probably wrong: the Wi-Fi or router, the internet, the Microsoft services for
    device management, or the company network (VPN). The rows are part of the copy, the export and the ticket.

  Settings
    Enabled    True switches the measurements on.
    Gateway    True (default) measures the round-trip time (ping) to the default gateway (group Local).
               Some firewalls drop ping: a gateway that does not answer while the internet works is not reported
               as "router down".
    Microsoft  True (default) measures the targets of the group Microsoft. False leaves that group out.
    HostNames  True (default) shows the host name and port of each target in the rows. False shows only the
               result, so that internal host names do not appear in the copy, the export and the ticket.
    Samples    Measurements per value, 1 to 5 (default 3).
    TimeoutMs  Wait time per measurement in milliseconds, 200 to 5000 (default 2000).

  Targets (up to 12)
    Label  Name shown in the list (letters, digits . _ - and space, up to 40 characters).
    Host   DNS name or IPv4 address. Choose the name according to what the measurement should show.
    Port   Optional (1 to 65535). With a port the time to open a TCP connection is measured too.
           Without a port only the name lookup is measured. For an IPv4 address no name lookup is measured, so
           an IPv4 address needs a port.
    Group  Optional. Which row the target belongs to:
           Local      a device in the local network, for example a printer or the router (the default gateway is
                      measured by itself with Gateway="True");
           Internet   a target that is reachable from the internet. The "Internet reachable" test of the system
                      information counts as one more Internet target. Use an address that you operate or that is
                      meant for such tests;
           Microsoft  a Microsoft service endpoint (see below);
           Company    a target that is reachable only in the company network or through VPN (intranet, file
                      server, domain controller).
           Without Group the target is measured and shown, but it does not count for the result.
    Kind   Optional, only with Group="Company". Vpn: the target is reachable through VPN (default for the hint
           "check the VPN"). Intranet: the target is reachable in the company network at the location; the hint
           then asks to check the network connection at the location instead of the VPN.
  Entries that are not valid are skipped. Check the file with tools\Test-SelfXNetworkMeasure.ps1.

  Microsoft endpoints delivered with SelfX (group Microsoft, TCP 443)
    Source: Microsoft Learn, "Network endpoints for Microsoft Intune"
    (https://learn.microsoft.com/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/intune-endpoints), checked on 2026-11-27.
    manage.microsoft.com               Intune client and host service (endpoint set 163)
    login.microsoftonline.com          Authentication and identity, Microsoft Entra ID (endpoint set 56)
    graph.windows.net                  Authentication and identity, Microsoft Entra ID (endpoint set 56)
    enterpriseregistration.windows.net Device registration (endpoint set 59)
  Switch the group off with Microsoft="False" or remove the targets. Microsoft changes this list from time to time;
  compare it with the article before you rely on it.

  Note on the DNS time: the name is resolved through the Windows resolver, so its cache applies. Repeated
  measurements can be faster than the first one.

  Example for your own targets (remove the comment marks):
    <Target Label="Router printer" Host="192.168.1.20" Port="9100" Group="Local" />
    <Target Label="Internet" Host="www.example.org" Port="443" Group="Internet" />
    <Target Label="Intranet" Host="intranet.example.org" Port="443" Group="Company" Kind="Vpn" />
    <Target Label="File server" Host="files.example.org" Port="445" Group="Company" Kind="Intranet" />

  Citrix: add the address of the StoreFront server or the Gateway of the Citrix Workspace app as a target
  (for example Label="Citrix Gateway" Host="citrix.example.org" Port="443" Group="Company"). The row shows whether
  the name resolves and how fast the connection opens; users and the support team see it in the system information
  and in the ticket.
-->
<Network_Test_Config>
  <Settings Enabled="False" Gateway="True" Microsoft="True" HostNames="True" Samples="3" TimeoutMs="2000" />
  <Targets>
    <Target Label="Intune service" Host="manage.microsoft.com" Port="443" Group="Microsoft" />
    <Target Label="Entra ID sign-in" Host="login.microsoftonline.com" Port="443" Group="Microsoft" />
    <Target Label="Entra ID directory" Host="graph.windows.net" Port="443" Group="Microsoft" />
    <Target Label="Device registration" Host="enterpriseregistration.windows.net" Port="443" Group="Microsoft" />
  </Targets>
</Network_Test_Config>
